<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Seongjong of Joseon</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1457">1457</a>–<a href="/years/1494">1494</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/15th-century">15th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Seongjong of Joseon was king of Joseon from 1469 to 1494. In 1485 he promulgated the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gyeongguk-daejeon">Gyeongguk daejeon</a>, the National Code that formed the legal basis of the Joseon state. He patronised Confucian scholarship and brought scholars of the rural sarim faction into office, and his reign is seen as a period of stability and cultural achievement.</p></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Seongjong, whose personal name was Yi Hyeol, was the ninth monarch of Joseon. He was born the second son of Crown Prince Yi Jang and Crown Princess Su of the Cheongju Han clan, and his father died a few months after his birth. He was a grandson of King Sejo, and in 1467 he married Han Song-yi, youngest daughter of Han Myŏnghoe.</p><p>Although he had an older brother and the late King Yejong had left a biological son, Jalsan was chosen as successor and was made the adopted son of Yejong and his second wife, Queen Han. After his accession his biological father was honoured with the temple name Deokjong, and his mother became queen with the honorary name Insu.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Reign</h2><p>Seongjong was only 12 when he was crowned, so his grandmother, Grand Queen Dowager Jaseong, ruled with his mother, Queen Insu, and his aunt and adoptive mother, Queen Dowager Inhye. In 1476, at the age of 19, he began to govern in his own name. After his first wife died in 1474 he promoted a concubine, Lady Yun, to queen. She was later executed for trying to poison other concubines and harm the king, which became a major reason for the tyranny of his successor, Yeonsangun.</p><p>His reign was marked by economic prosperity and growth, resting on the laws of Taejong, Sejong and Sejo. The Grand Code for State Administration, begun by his grandfather, was completed and put into effect, and he ordered revisions of it. He expanded the Office of Special Advisors (Hongmungwan), an advisory council and royal library, and strengthened the Three Offices (Samsa) as a check on the court. For the first time since <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sejong-the-great">Sejong the Great</a>, Confucian scholars whose views clashed with those of the conservative officials were brought to court, and he appointed able administrators regardless of their politics. He prohibited the construction of new monasteries and the ordination of new monks.</p><p>He encouraged books on geography, social etiquette and subjects that benefited the common people. The Widow Remarriage Ban of 1477 barred the sons of remarried women from public office, strengthening an existing stigma. In 1491 a campaign led by General Heo Jong drove the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jurchens">Jurchens</a> commanded by Udige north of the Amrok River.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Death and legacy</h2><p>Seongjong died in January and was buried south of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/seoul">Seoul</a>, in the tomb known as Seonneung, where his third wife, Queen Jeonghyeon, was interred 35 years later. He was succeeded by his son, Crown Prince Yi Yung, known as Yeonsangun.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Seongjong of Joseon” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gyeongbokgung">Gyeongbokgung</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/south-korea">South Korea</a>. Died in Changdeokgung, in what is now <a href="/countries/south-korea">South Korea</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=37.58,126.98&amp;year=1481" aria-label="Gyeongbokgung on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="7722 812 900 540" role="img" aria-label="Map: the birthplace of Seongjong of Joseon"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M8172 1082h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M8172 1082h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the birthplace. <a href="/atlas?at=37.58,126.98&amp;year=1481">What was here in 1481? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
